+## `deployHookUrl` is untrusted input
+
+`deployHookUrl` is a plain text field a customer fills in themselves, so
+`triggerDeployHook` treats it the way it'd treat any other attacker-controlled
+URL: before the first request and before following any redirect,
+`assertSafeDeployHookUrl` resolves the hostname and refuses to proceed unless
+every address it resolves to is ordinary public internet. That rules out
+loopback, the RFC1918 and CGNAT ranges, link-local (which is also where a
+cloud's instance-metadata endpoint lives — `169.254.169.254` on AWS, GCP and
+Azure alike), and non-`http(s)` schemes — otherwise this field is a way to get
+the API to make a request to its own internal network, or to whatever else it
+can reach, on a customer's behalf. A URL that fails this check is treated the
+same as an unreachable one: `deployTriggered: false`, no retries, since
+retrying a request this app has already decided not to make wouldn't change
+the outcome.
+
+This closes the direct case (a hostname or IP literal that resolves straight
+to a private address) and the redirect case (a hook that resolves safely but
+answers with a `3xx` pointing somewhere it shouldn't). It does not pin the
+connection to the address it validated, so a hostname whose DNS answer
+changes between that check and the `fetch` a moment later — a "DNS
+rebinding" attack — is a gap this doesn't close. That'd take an attacker
+running authoritative DNS for the hook's hostname with a very short TTL and
+precise timing against this app's own request; if that threat becomes real
+for this product, the fix is pinning the resolved address for the connection
+itself rather than re-checking it, which needs more than what's in Node's
+standard library today.
+
